  "notes": "The roughest-edged voice on 2000s radio — a Bakersfield-derived rasp over rock-leaning arrangements. The entry gives one explicit engine instruction and it drives the session block: \"snare hit hard with visible velocity variance — model higher humanisation than the polished Nashville norm,\" so `velocityVar` runs at 0.28 against a low quantize strength, well outside the section's usual grid discipline. The rest of the kit is the stated grammar: \"a rock-sized, roomy kit; kick on 1 and 'and-of-3,' 8th hats.\" The bass is quoted as \"a driving 8th-note feel and slight overdrive; locked to the kick,\" so `mirror_kick` at a high root-following probability. Harmony is \"I–IV–V, I–♭VII–IV, and vi–IV–I–V.\" Melody is \"mid-baritone with blues bends and flat-3/flat-7 emphasis, phrases starting behind the beat; rasp used as the ornament instead of melisma\" — hence the major_blues weight, the raised half-step dissonance and an octave-jump probability near zero. The arrangement note is negative and real: \"less dynamic staging than his peers — the records stay loud from bar 1,\" which is why there is no cross-stick verse here and the velocity floor sits high. ⚠ Confidence is medium, matching the research; the per-track readings are [LOW-CONF]. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are the 0.28 velocity variance itself, the perc lane set and the counter ratio.",
